          "low": 16,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, reducing flip-flop count and area versus fully pipelined, at the cost of higher latency (ceil(N/m)+2 cycles) and potentially lower Fmax due to longer combinational paths. For the batch constraint, we need ceil(N/m)+2 cycles to complete 32 results within 0.5us. Since pipelined_m still produces 1 result/cycle in steady state, the throughput is still Fmax. The key question is whether the initial latency plus 32 cycles fits in 0.5us. With m=2-4 and N=14-18, latency is ceil(14/2)+2=9 to ceil(18/4)+2=7 cycles, plus 32 cycles for the batch = ~41-49 cycles total. At 64MHz, 49 cycles = 766ns > 500ns, which may violate the constraint. At higher Fmax (e.g., 100MHz), 49 cycles = 490ns < 500ns. So pipelined_m with small m (2) and higher Fmax may work. The area savings from fewer registers make this family attractive for the min-area objective. Search m=2-4 to find the sweet spot between area and the batch latency constraint."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires throughput_msps >= 32, max_abs_err <= 2^-12, and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5us for 32 back-to-back requests. Only pipelined and pipelined_m produce 1 result/cycle, so they are the only families that can meet the throughput constraint (iterative and unrolled_k produce 1 result every several cycles, giving throughput = Fmax/k or Fmax/(N+3), which would require very high Fmax). For the batch constraint, 32 results must arrive within 0.5us of the tick. With 1 result/cycle, this requires Fmax >= 64 MHz (32 cycles in 0.5us) plus the pipeline latency. pipelined has N+2 latency; pipelined_m has ceil(N/m)+2 latency. Both can meet this if Fmax is high enough. The accuracy constraint (12 bits) is easily met with N >= 14 and data_width >= 16. The min-area objective favors smaller data_width, smaller n_iter, and pipelined_m (fewer registers). I split the budget 50/50 between the two families: pipelined is the baseline (simpler, higher Fmax, guaranteed to meet batch constraint at moderate Fmax), and pipelined_m explores area savings via reduced register count. Ranges are focused on the lower end of data_width and n_iter to minimize area while maintaining accuracy margin. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small (0-2) since 12-bit accuracy does not require large guard bits."
}

